      "role": "user",
      "content": "I get that. It's about the setting and the stakes. \n\nBut I guess I'm wondering about the \"naturalness\" of it. If I swapped the names—say, if Julian was 'Andre' and Marcus was 'Julian'—but kept the exact same descriptions (the docks, the philanthropist, the attorney), would the \"vibe\" change for an audience? \n\nIn other words, do you think audiences subconsciously associate certain types of lives—like the \"street-smart\" or \"neighborhood\" types—with specific races? I want to know if my writing is accidentally tapping into those automatic associations people have.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the swap could subtly shift the perceived vibe because names carry cultural baggage that informs initial audience assumptions. Audiences often make subconscious leaps, and pairing certain names with certain socioeconomic roles can trigger existing, sometimes biased, tropes. To be safe, rely on the *action* and *internal conflict* of the character to define their space, rather than relying solely on typical name-role associations.",
      "source": "target",
